 17592186045417] [17592186571370 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ecddd1b8-00da-5bd7-a5d9-f8af1c70264b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571371 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571371 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571371 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571371 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571371 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571371 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571371 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571371 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186571165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571371 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "edb95141-3ad9-54ec-a8b4-67b0e62cf950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186571314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571377 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=-8+6y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0342016a-166b-5391-a2e8-8b20bd450fa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571377 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8+6y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y+8 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=2$ and $y=4.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=2$ to $y=4.$\n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}y(-8+6y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571395 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186571370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571395 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105740" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571395 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571395 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=5{y}^{3}-5{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571395 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f112823-f777-5f74-ab7d-687fcdb73828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571395 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{3}-5{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{3}(1-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(5{y}^{3}-5{y}^{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =10\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{4}-{y}^{5})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =10\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{5}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =10\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{6}}{6}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =10\\pi ((\\frac{1}{5}-0)-(\\frac{1}{6}-0)) \\\\  & =10\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571417 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186571365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571417 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571417 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571417 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571417 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b1c2b26-9aae-5367-9691-64e9ca75fb9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571417 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}(2-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0,\\,2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186571330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571419 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571419 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571419 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}-5{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8025abae-d2ce-537b-9664-a4fb5d59e410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571419 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-5y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{5}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{5}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/5}y({y}^{2}-5{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/5}({y}^{3}-5{y}^{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1/5}-5\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{0}^{1/5}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4\\cdot {5}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{5}^{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{{5}^{4}}(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{20\\cdot {5}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\,250}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186571371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571430 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/af/af0f21f914c1a773cf80ba80a43547a382a1bf4007b1916cca8ce90f6b2d15b2.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e08ef03-042b-5ef4-bd8e-13473edc79ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571430 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(3-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0,\\,3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571492 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186571520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571492 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8d03fea6-c5c4-5d31-b587-b42678eb1cd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571492 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186571395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571493 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186571508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571493 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e96fcaf-3825-5afc-b540-f2f106da0fb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571493 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186571430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571499 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c08bf546-3c9a-5b7a-b09e-fcee